body:
**Doc-drift risk — no CI hook keeps the safety text in sync with behavior.**

The `goose_bridge_free_string` safety section correctly documents ownership transfer and the double-free pitfall. However, if the implementation changes (e.g. the allocation strategy behind `CString` changes, or a wrapper is added), these docs will silently become wrong.

Minimum mitigation: reference the exact implementation so a future maintainer editing the code knows to update the doc:
```rust
/// Releases the string by calling [`CString::from_raw`] on `value`,
/// which reclaims the memory via the Rust allocator that produced it
/// (see the `string_to_c_string` helper). Do not pass a pointer
/// allocated by any other means.
```

**Silent fallback swallows the real error.**

`.or_else(|_| GooseStore::open(path))` discards *why* `open_existing_current` failed. If it fails for I/O, lock contention, or corruption (not just a stale schema), you run a full migration against a possibly damaged DB with no log. Recommend logging the discarded error:

```rust
.or_else(|err| {
    log::warn!("open_existing_current failed ({err}), falling back to full open");
    GooseStore::open(path)
})
```

Also: the startup ordering assumption is currently implicit — the hot path works correctly only after a full `open()` (with `migrate()`) has run at least once. A comment here or in `GooseAppModel` documenting that dependency would prevent regressions if startup order ever changes.

**Retain cycle / lifetime inversion + threading.**

`GooseAppModel` (a `@StateObject` owned above this view) will hold `HealthDataStore` alive indefinitely via this closure — a lifetime inversion where the model layer pins a view-layer object. The closure is also never cleared when the view disappears.

Fix:
```swift
.onAppear {
    model.onHistoricalSyncCompleted = { [weak healthStore] in
        healthStore?.runPacketInputs()
    }
}
.onDisappear { model.onHistoricalSyncCompleted = nil }
```

Also confirm `handleHistoricalSyncProgress` is main-actor-isolated at the fire site — `runPacketInputs()` is `@MainActor` and must not be called from the BLE queue.
